We have an Alpha server running DU 4.0D which exports user's home
directories via NFS to various other Unix and Linux machines both on its
own subnet and on other subnets. This has worked well for years but
yesterday morning, in readiness for a network upgrade, RIP and proxy ARP
were turned off on the router feeding these subnets. We do not run gated
or routed and use a single static route so this should not have made any
difference.

Now we have the odd situation of NFS working fine where the client is on
the same subnet, but Linux machines on other subnets can't see the nfsd
UDP service. Attempts to actuially mount the NFS volume casues the
client to hang. But Unix clients on other subnets (eg D Unix, Solaris)
have no problems mounting NFS volumes.

Here's the output of rpcinfo from a Digital Unix machine on a different
subnet:

	rpcinfo -u geometry nfs 2
	program 100003 version 2 ready and waiting

	rpcinfo -t geometry nfs 2
	program 100003 version 2 ready and waiting

but from any Linux box we get:

	rpcinfo -u geometry nfs 2
	rpcinfo: RPC: Timed out
	program 100003 version 2 is not available

	rpcinfo -t geometry nfs 2
	program 100003 version 2 ready and waiting


It doesn't matter if we probe NFS version 3, we get the same result. And
other UDP services like NIS (this server is also a NIS master) check out
fine with both Unix & Linux:

(from the Linux box on a remote subnet):

	rpcinfo -t geometry ypserv 2
	program 100004 version 2 ready and waiting

Now, it can't be a Linux issue as several dozen SuSE, Red Hat and Debian
Linux systems can't all be misconfigured, and Linux clients mount fine on the
local subnet where the server is. And it all worked before. Also, these Linux
clients on remote subnets mount NFS exports from other DU servers on the same
subnet.

