Yesterday I posted the message below but I've now found the problem and
it's been fixed. I thought I'd describe the solution here in case others run
into the same problem in the future.

There appears to be a bug in the ifconfig that ships with DU 4.0D so that if
an interface is configured with primary IP address, netmask and alias IP all
on one line, what actually happens is that the IP addresses are configured
correctly but the broadcast addresses for both are the same as the alias IP
address. So if I use the netsetup script to set the tu0 interface up, it
places the following line into /.etc/rc.config:

   IFCONFIG_0="155.198.192.7 netmask 255.255.255.0 alias 155.198.192.59"

and at boot-up, the broadcast address is actually set to 155.198.192.59
instead of 155.198.192.255. This obviously affected correct operation of
RPC UDP calls from other subnets (eg, 155.198.190.0/24) - Linux NFS uses
UDP while DU and Solaris appear to use TCP for RPC - but I'm not too sure
of the mechanisms involved or what exactly failed.

Manually reconfiguring the correct broadcast addresses after boot time
and running '/usr/sbin/rcinet restart' didn't fix the problem as far as the
portmapper was concerned but removing the alias argument from the interface
configuration in /etc/rc.config and rebooting got everything working again.
A separate script in /sbin/rc3.d now runs towards the end of the boot process
(after everything else has been set up and has stabilised) to assign the IP
alias to the tu0 interface:

	/usr/sbin/ifconfig tu0 alias 155.198.192.59/24


Andy


