> Any one else remember when DECUS was free information exchange?

I think you would be hard pressed to find a intellectually honest person who
could ever remember it being free.  Many things the User Group did was at no
charge to the membership thanks to healthy subsidies from Digital covering
production and operations costs.  On a worldwide basis that was about $10
million dollars per year in the very early 1990's.  That is 1990 dollars
which would be about $20 million in today's dollars.  Leaving aside the
issue of the CD's it is a myth that DECUS was free information exchange.
What it was, for the intellectually honest, was the exchange of information
and the cost of that was subsidized by Digital.  Of course that was also in
the era of fat gross margins on every system that went out the door.

To the CD issue - we sold so few of them that we lost money on them.  What
has not been made public is the price increase for the DECUS Symposium in
1997 was due to the cost of CD's.  We made decision that we could not afford
the loss on the CD's anymore and therefore we increased the price of the
Symposium and gave them away to all attendees.  In effect we forced everyone
to buy one.  One of the business realities is there is a hidden cost to
maintaining an order fulfillment system.  There is a magic point at which is
less expensive to ship a CD to everyone versus the overhead cost of
maintaining a order entry system.

If one goes back to 1996 and 1997 one will find postings from people
complaining about the turn around time for the CD's.  We made a business
decision that the best way to solve this problem was to put the
presentations on-line.  While not everyone will be happy with that business
decision we believe for the resources available it made the information
available to most folks in the fastest possible manner.

Encompass, CUO, ITUG, OV Forum, and Interex are all both businesses and User
Groups.  As businesses they all need to deal with business realities.  I
suspect that if Encompass could sell an additional 100 memberships it could
supply every member a CD of the Symposium hand-outs.  However, the data from
the past suggests that while this is a hot issue for few - it is something
that is only an issue for a few...


--

