So here's a scenario....

HP spins off the Alpha- VMS group into a separate company (for this
discussion's sake, let's call the new company 'Digital Equipment
Corporation' or 'DEC' for short) and issue those shares to existing HP
shareholders.

Right now in today's market, such an action would probably not affect HP's
stock price by more than a few cents per share. That may seem unusual but
since nobody in the financial analyst community seems to think much about
VMS or its revenue/profit stream (and HP could spin this as being a drag on
profits just as easily as being a contributor to profitability), it *could*
have no material affect on HP's current stock price.

This would being immediate benefits to HP:
1) No more customers complaining about the fate of their o/s and hardware.
2) Gartner would applaud them for focusing on 'winning' operating systems.
3) No more distractions internally.
4) No DOJ probe - eventually.
5) No more COE-DII committment to worry about.
6) No 'roadmap' to follow.
7) A *perceived* insignificant change in the number of IA-64 systems HP will
sell.


As to DEC, the pro's and cons would be:
1) Freedom to market the product.
2) Ability to support/port to any chip architecture they desire.
3) A FAB-less enterprise.
4) Ability to recover key former employees.
5) Gartner damning them to eternal hell.
6) Possible funding problems in the capital markets.
7) Estimated stock price of $2-6 range to begin with.
8) Doubts about continued viability.
9) Have to build the Services side from scratch.


But it won't happen because it would put a real competitor back into the
market.




