,Nate Johnson <natejohn@indiana.edu> said:
> That's interresting.  I have both of those configurations set.
> xlogin is turned off, so xdm and cde aren't on the keyboard/mouse
> console, and the SRM variable "console" is set to serial, so I think
> that /dev/console is really referring to the serial port console
> connection.  What I actually see on the monitor is just a blinking
> cursor, and keyboard input is not accepted.  See what I mean about
> needing console at both variables "graphics" and "serial"?
>
Ahh,
Try this.. Disable x-logins by taking away the startup script in
/sbin/init.d
Leave the console variable set to graphics.
OK So far you have console logins on the local keboard and screen.
Now set up an entry in inittab for a getty, make it just like the
one for the console that is already there, then change it to point
at the serial port you want.
Lastly if you want your serial logins to be 'trusted' in the same
way that the console is trusted you need to make some entries in
/etc/securettys and in the auth terminal database.
Sorry to be a bit vague about this but I don't have a test system
here at home.
Remember to get yourself a separate boot media (Like the install
CD) before you change inittab, if you screw it up your system
won't boot.
Adam




