I think your concept of mounting is a little skewed.  You don't mount a 
directory on a disk.  You mount a prepared filesystem (or a 
filedomain#fileset for AdvFS or a logical volume for LSM) to a 
directory, and that directory must (or I should say ought to) be 
otherwise unused.  /tmp is used for all kinds of "stuff" on a running 
system, in fact there may be several things already running that keep 
the directory used/open, perhaps entirely invisible to you at first 
glance.  That you can't mount a filesystem to /tmp does not surprise me 
in the least, in fact it sounds quite correct.  There are several ways 
around this, having as much to do with personal preference as with 
specific site or machine requirements.  You might want to come back in 
and tell us exactly what you're trying to accomplish.

QH



