In article <e5e615f2.0210140252.77ad4614@posting.google.com>,
 gerard.holmes@mail.esb.ie (Ger) wrote:

X Hi,
X  I'm trying to mount /tmp on a new disk, but I keep getting "Device
X Busy" message.In fact when I type mount -a, I get the same message for
X all the filesystems.How can I make the Devices un-busy?
X 
X Rgds
X Ger

You mean you are trying to

        mount /dev/dsk/dsk27c /tmp

of course after you have built a file system on /dev/dsk/dsk27c

The "mount /tmp" syntax does work if you have your /etc/fstab file setup 
correctly.

The device busy error would be referring to the physical device.  In my 
example this would be /dev/dsk/dsk27c, _NOT_ /tmp.  You can have files 
open in /tmp and still mount something over top of it.

Generally the busy error is because someone else is already using 
/dev/dsk/dsk27c as a file system.

Check to make sure it is not already in use.  fuser might help, but also 
look for overlapping partitions, and see if it is part of an AdvFS 
domain by looking in /etc/fdmns/*/*

Also it could be in use by LSM (not sure how to check if LSM owns it, 
but that is also a possibility).

                                        Bob Harris


