I am running a server Compaq DS10 with digital UNIX. The server is
deployed in network with several subnets separated by routers and
firewall. Recently I found a problem when people were trying to
download a data from different network subnets. The FTP transfer was
really slow and sometimes got stuck. A have a closer look at the data
traffic with sniffer and I realised that a low packet loss (up to 8%)
happened time to time during the transfer. What amazed me was the
server reaction when such a packet loss begun:
1. server sent couple of packets 
2. clent did not recieved them all, and asked server for
retransmission (by ACK with appropriate sequence number)
3. server received the ACK, but ignored it and continued sending the
data
4. client repeated the ACK twice (or more)
5. finally server stopped sending anything for 1,7 to 3 seconds and
then sent requested retransmission.

The sequence is very similiar or the same when other packet was lost.
The reason for timeout is not clear to me.
When FTP client is located in the same subnet with server, FTP
transfer is working fine and very fast (with no packet loss of
course).

Has anybody seen this problem before?
Thanks for any hints
 Libor Bucinsky


