> Actually, threads of one process can be spread across cpus and rads.
> 
> You can easily know what CPU is scheduled for one thread, and then
> you'll know the RAD.
> 
> ps -emf -o pid,cputime,user,state,pcpu,psr,comm,vsz,rss
> 
> psr is the CPU number, ~ means hard affinity, man ps for details
> 
> # vmstat -R 1     will tell you memory list status per RAD. You can
> see if one RAD is overflowing memory allocation to other RADs.
> 
> To know *process* memory allocation across rads can be interesting as
> well. Try to find 'cda' utility, and then, for each process pid you
> want to know RAM allocation :
> # cda
> cda> numa_info 'pid'
> 
> Cheers




