> Yep, I am fairly new to this *nix admin stuff, and taking notes as I
> go...
>
> Anyway, I have a DEC 3000/400 running D/UX 3.2C, and a Sun Ultra1
> Creator running Solaris 9. Both default installs.
> At the Solaris remote login screen, the DEC shows up with the 'Server
> Information' field showing 'Willing To Manage'. When I choose the entry
> for the Dec, and hit Okay, the D/UX login background shows up, with the
> message of "Cannot obtain database information on this terminal". The
> Acknowledge button only brings up a blank red letter warning box, with
> another Ack, that brings me back around to the first message. Back and
> forth it goes, so I have tto forcibly boot the Ultra to recover...
>
> Seems toi me like something in /etc/termcap needs to be added/tweaked,
> but I'm not positive. Or am I missing something else?
>
> And I'm just kinda curious, how would I be able to do the reverse of
> this, graphically log into the Ultra from D/UX? Hmmm...

David,

1.) You are getting warm, but this sounds like X-windows, (specifically
XDM/CDE) not a tty, so you need to add information into a different file.

(I am quoting from experience on 4.0D-G here, but I assume 3.2C is the
same.)

a. Add a terminal entry into the terminal database:

/usr/tcb/bin/edauth -d t -s </myfile

Where myfile contains an entry similar to the following (terminal name here
is the name of your solaris box):

terminalname\:0\
    :t_devname=terminalname\:0:t_xdisplay:chkent:

b. Use Dxdevices to add the solaris box to the known list of X devices:

/usr/tcb/bin/dxdevices

From the menu, select Devices, then Create

Enter the hostname of the solaris box with :0 appended, e.g.

terminalname:0

Select the 'X Display' radio button and click the 'Ok' button.

In the Device List box, enter the hostname again with :0 appended and with
:0.0 appended, e.g.

terminalname:0
terminalname:0.0

Leave other settings at default values, and press the 'Ok' button.

Then exit from Dxdevices.

Now try to access the solaris box again.

This worked for me.

2.) It should be possible to do the reverse as well, but it is 5 years since
I have looked closely at Solaris, so it will have changed from what I know.

Regards,

Howard Anderson.









