Well, for starters you would have to mention which version of the OS you
are using. Things like that change.

Which brings me to my next point. Although it is possible to get things
like the "current process' PID", the definition of current process is at
best vague at certain points in the kernel. If you are servicing a system
call or some other such thing, then the current process is pretty well
defined. If you are in an ISR, the current process means little to you.

Segue into my next next point. Because such things change frequently, we do
not document them to avoid having compatibility problems with drivers.  The
interfaces and variables we document are "guaranteed". Those we don't aren't. 
And as for "all the global symbols", there are thousands. 99.9% of them mean
nothing to anyone except some small piece of code.

