We use to have, internally in Compaq, that kind of paper. I'm not sure
they are disclosable outside.

Point one : depending on who made the test, the leader changed.
Point two : globally, performance was more or less similar, when IO
structure was coherent. I mean, in the near past, the main limiting
factor was the HBA bandwidth - 90 Mbytes/s FC per Fiber - , and
physical disk bandwidth - Around 40 Mbytes/s per disk -.

The CPU usage of LSM was low -under 5% overhead- and generally not
noticeable in normal operation. In case of reconstruction, more CPU
load and most of all, I/O bandwidth is stolen on the system. This can
be painful, but joyfully, a rare operation.


HW Raid is very easy to manage, and IMHO, must be chosen in non IO
bandwidth bound application. But getting rid of SPOF can lead to
argument there.

Combination of HW Raid and LSM is excellent and scalable in high
bandwidth demand application. With multipathing, prefer mirroring by
HW, and stripe with LSM, you have no SPOF, and reconstruction of a
failed disk will be nearly at no pain.


Only my opinion, cheers,

Emmanuel


