In article <58ba0101.0210010407.62a9e4a0@posting.google.com>, andrew.rycroft@intrinsitech.com (Andrew Rycroft) writes:

>Is there any information on the comparative performance between LSM,
>and hardware RAID ?

What RAID levels?  What version of the operating system and LSM?

Depending on what you're trying to get there no reason you can't
use both.  Subsystem based RAID often has the advantage of being
easy to manage and it offloads nearly all the complexity of its
use from the host.  Subsystems that offer failover, might expect
the host to handle part of it; multi-path support in recent
versions of Tru64 UNIX and HSG80/HSV110 based systems.

However, in many configurations, the data path to the subsystem
may be a single point of failure.  Where availability is a concern,
using LSM to mirror the data across multiple subsystems can help
prevent this problem.  From a performance point of view, the
subsystem has its own performance limit that be less than what
the host can consume under load.  Using LSM to spread out the
workload over multiple subsystems may allow higher aggregate
performance than you can get out of a single subsystem.
 
>
>Or the amount of overhead to the system LSM adds since it is now
>handling the RAID ?

RAID-1 is fairly cheap if you can tolerate the extra I/O load.  RAID-5
is bound to be more expensive, but done well, the I/O load is likely
to be the bottleneck and not the CPU load.  Regeneration is bound to
slow things down, but the presumption is that this is better than
not having access to the data at all and that you won't be using it
longer than necessary.
