I agree with the last reply but would like to add a couple more
points.

Make sure you fully invest in understanding how LSM works before you
bein to use it.  Simulate failures and have documented tested
procedures for dealing with them before you use LSM in production. 
The best docs are the V4 docs (available online) because they contain
very good examples in one of the appendices.

I have seen LSM increase performance merely by its presence.  In other
words, make one volume out of one plex out of one subdisk out of an
entire disk and performance will go up with miniscule CPU cost.  I'm
not sure if it's due to LSM's buffering or pre-packaging IO into
pre-arranged slices but it does work.

For ultimate performance I hardware-stripe and then use LSM to mirror
the stripes.  Nasty to recover from if there's a problem but this is a
benchmark scenario rather than production where you need 24x7.  You
can also use LSM to stripe RAID5 sets which can get you pretty decent
performance and at the same time provide some fault resilience at the
hardware level.

I have always been a huge fan of LSM.  Once I bit the bullet, learned
how it worked, and put it to use, I never once looked back.  The only
down side, as I said before, is understanding it, especially when a
spindle bites the dust, but this is an exceedingly-small price to pay
for such a capable utility.

QH.


