I agree that improper speed negotiation is a likely culprit.  Also,
what do the status lights on the 100Mb switches tell you regarding
usage and port speed?  Are you saying that other connections between
the same 2 machines slow down at the same time the file xfer is being
done?  Is the other machine 100Mb as well?  Would it be possible to
connect the 2 machines with a crossover cable and thus bypass the
switch, just as a test (I'm not a network expert so I don't even know
if this is do-able)?  Are you exceeding any specs regarding cable
length or switch hops?  Do FTP, Samba, and NFS all give you pretty
much the same result (I believe you said yes but I just want to be
sure)?  Are you sure the switch is ok?  Is there a chunk of 10Mb
somewhere between the 2 systems (I once saw someone replace a bad
100Mb switch with a cheap 10Mb hub while waiting for a replacement,
and he told no one)?  Has anyone made any network changes recently
that you might not have been informed of (a VERY common problem in the
real world)?  Possible bad/flaky cable?  What happens with a transfer
of one mfs file on one system to an mfs file on another system (this
would eliminate AdvFS and actuator waits as potential problems)?  You
may also want to run collect while the problem is present and then
play back the collect data file with collgui to see if anything pops
up.  Also check EVM for anything weird in the event log possibly
pointing to flaky hardware.

Just some random thoughts.......... good luck.

QH


